Step 1: Open Your GitHub Repository
Log in to GitHub.
Open your repository.

Example:

https://github.com/<username>/<repository>

You will see:

Code
Issues
Pull Requests
Actions
Projects
Wiki
Security
Insights
Settings
Step 2: Go to Repository Settings

Click

Settings

located at the top-right of the repository navigation bar.

Step 3: Open Environments

From the left navigation menu, scroll to Code and automation and click:

Environments

You should see a page similar to:

Environments

No environments yet.

[ New environment ]
Step 4: Create the Production Environment

Click:

New environment

Enter the environment name:

Production

Click:

Configure environment

The environment is now created.

Step 5: Configure Deployment Protection Rules

On the Production environment page, locate Deployment protection rules.

Enable:

✔ Required reviewers

This tells GitHub to pause deployments until someone approves them.

Step 6: Add Required Reviewers

Click:

Add reviewers

You can choose:

Individual GitHub users
GitHub Teams (recommended for organizations)

Examples:

Cloud Team Lead
DevOps Manager
Infrastructure Team
Platform Team

Select one or more reviewers and click Save protection rules.

Note: In a personal repository, you can only add collaborators. GitHub Teams are available in GitHub organizations.

Step 7: (Optional) Prevent Self-Approval

For better governance, enable:

Prevent self-review

This ensures the person who triggered the deployment cannot approve it themselves.

Step 8: (Optional) Restrict Deployment Branches

Under Deployment branches, choose one of the following:

All branches

or

Selected branches

If you choose Selected branches, add:

main

or

release/*

This prevents deployments from unauthorized branches.

Step 9: Save the Configuration

Click:

Save protection rules

Your Production environment is now protected.

Step 10: Reference the Environment in Your Workflow

In your GitHub Actions workflow, specify the environment:

jobs:
  terraform-apply:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    environment:
      name: Production

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

When this job starts, GitHub will pause it and request approval.

Step 11: Trigger the Workflow

Trigger the workflow by:

Pushing to the configured branch
Merging a pull request
Running it manually using Run workflow

The workflow will execute until it reaches the job that uses the Production environment.

Step 12: Review the Pending Deployment

Go to:

Repository
└── Actions
    └── <Workflow Name>

The workflow will show a status similar to:

Waiting for approval

Deployment to Production

The deployment job is paused until an authorized reviewer approves it.

Step 13: Approve the Deployment

An authorized reviewer should:

Open the workflow run.
Click Review deployments.
Select the Production environment.
Optionally enter a comment.
Click Approve and deploy.

Once approved, the workflow resumes automatically.

Step 14: Monitor the Deployment

After approval, the workflow continues with the remaining steps, such as:

Terraform Init
Terraform Apply
Infrastructure Deployment

The Actions page will update in real time until the deployment completes.
